body:
`ContactTracing` only exposed a low-level indexed API (`get_contact(agent, idx)`), making it cumbersome to iterate all contacts for an agent grouped by who they contacted and when. This adds a higher-level `get_contacts()` API matching the pattern described in the issue.

## New API

**`ContactRecord`** — new class returned by `get_contacts()`:
- `get_contact_id()` → `size_t` — id of the contacted agent  
- `get_times()` → `const std::set<int>&` — all simulation days the pair made contact

**`ContactTracing::get_contacts(size_t agent)`** → `const std::vector<ContactRecord>&`  
Groups the circular buffer contents by `contact_id`, collecting all recorded days per unique contact into a `std::set<int>`. Result is cached per-agent and invalidated automatically on the next `add_contact()` call for that agent.

```cpp
auto & ct = model.get_contact_tracing();
for (auto & agent : model.get_agents())
{
    for (const auto & contact : ct.get_contacts(agent.get_id()))
    {
        size_t contact_id = contact.get_contact_id();
        const std::set<int> & when = contact.get_times(); // e.g. {0, 5, 20}
    }
}
```

## Implementation

- **`contacttracing-bones.hpp`**: Added `ContactRecord` class; added private `cached_contacts` (`vector<vector<ContactRecord>>`) and `up_to_date` (`vector<bool>`) members; declared `get_contacts()`.
- **`contacttracing-meat.hpp`**: Constructors and `reset()` initialize cache members; `add_contact()` sets `up_to_date[agent_a] = false`; `get_contacts()` lazily rebuilds via `std::map<size_t, std::set<int>>` grouping.
- **`tests/30b-contact-tracing.cpp`**: Tests correct grouping, multi-day aggregation, empty agents, cache identity (same address on repeated calls), cache invalidation, and cross-verification against the existing `get_contact()` API.

Currently, the `ContactMatrix` class stores the data in an array of size nagents x max contacts. We need to have a new method that allows us to interact with a particular individual's contacts. I imagine the following pattern:

```cpp
auto ct = model.get_contact_tracing();
for (a: model.get_agents())
{
  for (auto & contact: ct.get_contacts(a))
  {

    // Days contacted
    auto contact_id = c->get_contact_id();
    std::vector< set > when = c->get_times(); 

  }
}
```

The `contact` iterator would retrieve information stored in `ContactMatrix`. This information can stored in `std::vector< std::set<int> >`, where each `std::set<int>` contains the dates when the corresponding agent contacted another agent. So, in our example, we could have:

- `a.get_id()` is 3.
- `contact_id` could be 5.
- And `when` could be `{0, 5, 20}` (so they saw each other on days 0, 5, and 20).

The `std::vector< std::set< int > >` can be re-computed every time that we call `get_times()`, but cached as a function of the last time that a contact was added to the agent. In other words, we can have a member object `std::vector< bool > ContactMatrix::up_to_date` that is default `false` and is switched to `true` once `get_times()` is called. The element in the vector is switched to `false` automatically every time that `ContactMatrix::add_contact()` is called.
